### One test, run alone on a fork

The ranking says `{{ flaky.tests.0.name }}` fails with no change to the code. To check that away from the project's own pull requests, we forked the repository to [{{ probe.repo }}](https://github.com/{{ probe.repo }}) and pushed one branch, `{{ probe.branch }}`, from commit `{{ static.ref | sha }}`. The branch adds one workflow file and changes nothing else. On a Linux, a Windows and a macOS runner it builds the SDK with the same steps as `ci.yml`. Then it runs the test alone repeatedly in one process, runs it alone 200 times spread over the runner's cores, and runs all of `tests/worker/test_worker.py` once the way CI does. No test is skipped, retried or changed.

We ran it twice. The first run gave the one-process step 200 repetitions and a 20-minute limit, which was too tight to finish. The second used 60 repetitions and longer limits. Both are in the table.

{{ table:flaky_probe }}

Query: `python -m cidiag query probe`. "Passed" and "Failed" are pytest's own counts where the step finished, and counts of its progress marks where it didn't.

- **The test fails when run alone.** In the {{ probe.steps_alone_finished }} single-test steps that ran to the end, {{ probe.finished_failed }} of {{ probe.finished_total }} repetitions failed. Of the {{ probe.errors_total }} failures pytest printed an error for, {{ probe.errors.0.count }} read `{{ probe.errors.0.error }}`, the same line as in the project's history.
- **One run of the test file doesn't show it.** The whole file passed in all {{ probe.file_steps }} of its steps: {{ probe.file_passed }} test results and {{ probe.file_failed }} failures. Someone who runs the file once will probably see nothing wrong.
- **On Windows the same stall takes a different shape.** On Linux and macOS, pytest-timeout enforces the 60-second limit with a signal and fails the one test. Windows has no such signal, so pytest-timeout uses a watchdog thread there (the logs say `timeout method: thread`), and when a test runs past the limit it ends the whole pytest process. {{ probe.windows_alone_finished }} of the {{ probe.windows_alone }} single-test steps on Windows ran to the end. In {{ probe.windows_ended_by_timeout }}, pytest-timeout ended the process with no summary and no test named. {{ probe.windows_step_limit }} went quiet until our step limit, and the log doesn't say why.

A Windows step that goes quiet with no test named resembles the {{ flaky.timeouts_windows }} Windows time-limit failures in the project's history (next subsection). In the ranking above, `{{ flaky.tests.0.name }}` is named {{ flaky.tests.0.failures }} times on Linux and macOS and {{ flaky.tests.0.on_windows }} times on Windows. Taken together, that suggests some of the Windows time-limit failures are this test, or others like it, reported in a different way.

The probe doesn't prove that. It ran one test, on a later day, on runners assigned to our fork. Repeating a test inside one pytest session is also not how CI runs it, and the repetition may add stalls of its own. What it does show is that this test can run past 60 seconds with no pull request involved. It did so on Linux and macOS in the steps that finished, and on Windows in the step pytest-timeout ended.

### Steps that ran past their time limit

In {{ flaky.by_kind.timeout }} failed-then-passed executions the log names no test. A test step reached the `timeout-minutes` set for it in the workflow file and the runner stopped it.

{{ table:flaky_timeouts }}

Query: `python -m cidiag query flaky.timeouts`. By runner: {{ flaky.timeouts_by_platform | counts }}. {{ flaky.timeouts_recent }} of the {{ flaky.by_kind.timeout }} started on or after {{ flaky.timeouts_recent_from }}.

The project sets `timeout = 60` and `timeout_func_only = true` for pytest in `pyproject.toml`. With that setting the 60-second limit covers the body of each test and leaves out its fixtures, so a stall while a fixture sets up or tears down would run until the step limit with no test named. That fits what the logs show. We didn't establish that it's the cause. Our stored copy of each log keeps the failure lines and drops pytest's progress output, so it can't say whether one test stalled or the whole step ran slow.

