### What this evidence shows and what it doesn't

- **It shows** that each failure counted above didn't come from the commit alone. The same job, on the same commit, later passed.
- **A pass after a failure suggests a flaky test without proving one.** It says the failure isn't deterministic. One occurrence could be a slow runner or a network fault. `{{ flaky.tests.0.name }}` failed this way {{ flaky.tests.0.failures }} times in {{ flaky.tests.0.runs }} runs on two operating systems, which is hard to explain as chance. The {{ flaky.tests_once }} tests that appear once are candidates, and we wouldn't act on one of them without seeing it again.
- **It undercounts.** A failure is counted only if someone re-ran the job and it then passed. {{ flaky.rerun_share_of_failed | pct }} of the runs with a failed attempt were re-run. Failures on `{{ config.branch }}` were never re-run. A flaky failure that the author cleared by pushing another commit is invisible here.
- **It doesn't say why a test is flaky.** The error line says what the test saw. Finding the cause means reading the test and the code under it, which a diagnostic doesn't do.
- **A step past its time limit names no test.** The {{ flaky.by_kind.timeout }} time-limit failures could be one stalled test or many.
- **The ranking covers 30 days.** A test that fails one time in several hundred runs may not appear at all, and GitHub keeps job logs for 90 days by default, so the window can't be pushed back far.
- **The project has its own reporting.** The workflow uploads JUnit XML for every test job. A team with those files can count failures per test directly, without waiting for someone to press re-run. We didn't use them because the artifacts expire after 14 days.

